Q: How do you find duplicate records in SQL?

One line:
GROUP BY the column(s) that define uniqueness, then HAVING COUNT(*) > 1.

────────────────────────────────────────
1️⃣ Duplicates on one column (email)

SELECT email, COUNT(*) AS cnt
FROM users
GROUP BY email
HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;

→ emails that appear more than once.

────────────────────────────────────────
2️⃣ Duplicates on multiple columns (name + phone)

SELECT name, phone, COUNT(*) AS cnt
FROM users
GROUP BY name, phone
HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;

────────────────────────────────────────
3️⃣ Show the actual duplicate rows (not just keys)

SELECT u.*
FROM users u
JOIN (
    SELECT email
    FROM users
    GROUP BY email
    HAVING COUNT(*) > 1
) d ON u.email = d.email
ORDER BY u.email;

────────────────────────────────────────
4️⃣ Interview follow-up: delete duplicates (keep lowest id)

-- MySQL idea: keep MIN(id), delete rest
DELETE u
FROM users u
JOIN users u2
  ON u.email = u2.email
 AND u.id > u2.id;

Or:
DELETE FROM users
WHERE id NOT IN (
    SELECT id FROM (
        SELECT MIN(id) AS id
        FROM users
        GROUP BY email
    ) t
);

(Always backup / run SELECT first in production.)

────────────────────────────────────────
Prevent future duplicates:
UNIQUE KEY / UNIQUE INDEX on email (or name+phone composite unique).

Interview answer:
“I group by the business key and use HAVING COUNT(*) > 1. For composite duplicates I group by those columns together.
To inspect rows I join back to the base table; to stop recurrence I add a unique constraint.”
